 [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481326 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481333 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481343 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481364 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the change of variables $u={e}^{x}$ and $v={e}^{y},$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 4\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481371 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481389 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481410 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481418 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481423 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481424 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481429 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-1),$ $(2,0),$ $(3,-2),$ and $(2,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481430 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481431 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481432 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481434 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759